  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nBy Sunday, the mid level shear is further suppressed and nudged SW\nby a TUTT Low reaching the TX coast. Some mid level dynamics are\nexpected to spin up over the Coastal Bend, leading to a secondary\nwave of pooled mid-level moisture moving through the area. This\nwave of moisture should not have as much mid to high cloudiness to\ncontaminate daytime rain chances, so we might just see our best\nrain chances overall to happen on daytime Sunday. Even if it plays\nout that way, we are only talking about most PoPs increasing to\n20-30 percent versus most areas seeing 10-20 percent today through\nSaturday. And of course, the Coastal Bend is where PoPs are\nhighest, so out SE CWA could see PoPs as high as 50 percent. \n\nAfter Sunday, the moisture in the surface to mid levels remains\nelevated for several days, but not enough to generate much more \nthan a few storms. This means we should continue to see some\ndaytime convection but probably mostly associated with the sea-\nbreeze and focused over eastern areas.\n\nYesterday's deterministic models held more optimism the pair of\nweak disturbances passing through would put a dent into the\nsubtropical upper ridge. That now looks to be a slight outlier, as\nthe past couple runs depict another round of days with upper highs\nfrom the mid 90s to near 102, and heat index values pushing close\nto borderline Heat Advisory conditions each day. The blended\nguidance maxes only fluctuated a degree or two so we'll stick \nwith moderated highs one more day in keeping our more populated \nareas of the I-35 Corridor just shy of 100; a shift to favor the \nwarmer MOS highs actually reaching 100 may happen in the next day \nor so, but we want to make sure the warming values are a trend and\nnot just a wobble.",
